---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const diffHref = toolPathBySlug('date-difference-calculator');
const ageHref = toolPathBySlug('age-calculator');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>What due date does the last period give? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Add 280 days to the first day of the last period when the cycle is 28 days. That is the
      Naegele count this page uses. For example, a last period on 1 January 2026 is due on
      8 October 2026. Count the same period on 1 March 2026 and the page says 8 weeks and 3
      days, still the first trimester, with 221 days left before 8 October.
    </p>
    <p>
      Change the cycle and only the due date moves. For example, a 32 day cycle from that same
      January day is due on 12 October 2026, four days later. The week count stays 8 weeks and
      3 days, because weeks are counted from the last period, not from the adjusted due date. The note
      under the date says the page used 32 and that the date moved four days later than a 28
      day cycle. At 28 that note stays hidden.
    </p>
    <p>
      A chat reply can recite "280 days." It will not keep the last period, the cycle length,
      the count date, and the trimester boundaries on one screen while you edit one of them.
      The arithmetic stays in the browser. Runs entirely on your device. Nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open the Pregnancy Due Date Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="naegele">
    <h2>How does the Naegele rule use cycle length? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The usual statement is one year minus three months plus seven days from the first day of
      the last period. What the Naegele rule counts, for a 28 day cycle, is about 280 days,
      and 280 days is what this page adds. The month shortcut and the day count disagree when a month is short. 31
      January plus one year, minus three months, plus seven days is not the same civil date as
      31 January plus 280 days. The page keeps the day count so a leap day and a 31 day month
      cannot drift the result.
    </p>
    <p>
      2026 is not a leap year, so 1 January plus 280 days is 8 October. For example, 1 January
      2024 plus 280 days is 7 October 2024, because 2024 is a leap year and 29 February sits
      inside the span. The due date comes one calendar day earlier than the non-leap year.
      The page does not round that away.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cycle length is the other input. The field accepts 21 to 45 days. Each day above 28
      adds one day to the 280. Each day below 28 subtracts one. For example, a 21 day cycle
      from 1 January 2026 is due 1 October 2026, seven days earlier, and the note says seven
      days earlier. A 45 day cycle would move the date 17 days later. The week count and the trimester
      dates do not take that adjustment. They still start at the last period.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="The cycle moves the due date only">
      <p>
        Weeks pregnant stay anchored to the first day of the last period. A longer cycle does
        not make you further along. It makes the estimated delivery date later, by the number
        of days the cycle differs from 28.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="weeks">
    <h2>How many weeks pregnant am I? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Subtract the last period from the date you are counting, in whole days, and split that
      span into weeks and leftover days. 1 January 2026 to 1 March 2026 is 59 days. 59 divided
      by 7 is 8 weeks, with 3 days left over. The card reads "8 weeks, 3 days." The same span
      with a 32 day cycle is still 8 weeks and 3 days. What changed is the due date, from 8
      October to 12 October, and the days remaining, from 221 to 225.
    </p>
    <p>
      Leave "Weeks pregnant on" blank and the page uses today. Fill it when you want a past
      or future day: a midwife appointment next Tuesday, or the day a test line appeared.
      The last period has to be on or before that date. A later period is rejected, because a
      negative week count would be a swapped pair of dates, not a pregnancy.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the due date itself the count is 40 weeks exactly for a 28 day cycle, because 280
      divided by 7 is 40. Two days later, 10 October 2026, the page says 2 days past due and
      40 weeks and 2 days, in the third trimester. Past the due date is allowed. The date you
      type is the date the week count uses.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Today is a date, not a guess">
      <p>
        Blank means today on this device. A worked example pins both dates so the week count
        cannot change overnight. 1 January 2026 counted on 1 March 2026 is always 8 weeks and
        3 days.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="trimesters">
    <h2>When do the trimester dates fall? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      This page starts the second trimester at 14 weeks, which is 98 days after the last
      period, and the third at 28 weeks, which is 196 days. From 1 January 2026 the second
      trimester starts on 9 April 2026 and the third starts on 16 July 2026. The due date for
      a 28 day cycle is 8 October 2026, which is inside the third trimester. Those two start
      dates are printed on the trimester card so you do not have to add 98 and 196 yourself.
    </p>
    <p>
      The milestones under the result mark each of those dates reached or not, using the same
      count date as the week card. On 1 March 2026 none of them have arrived. On 10 October
      2026 all three have. A 32 day cycle does not slide 9 April or 16 July. It only slides
      8 October to 12 October. If you expected the trimesters to move with the due date, the
      card is telling you the page did not do that.
    </p>
    <p>
      Estimated conception is a separate card. It is the cycle length minus 14 days after the
      last period. A 28 day cycle puts it on day 14, so 1 January 2026 gives 15 January 2026.
      A 32 day cycle gives 19 January 2026. The label says estimated. It is the same
      assumption a calendar method uses, and it is not a hormone test.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Which mistakes move the date? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Leaving a longer cycle at 28">
      <p>
        A 32 day cycle counted as 28 lands four days early: 8 October instead of 12 October
        in the January 2026 example. The note appears only after you type 32. If the field
        still says 28, the page is doing what you asked, and the date is the early one.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Expecting the weeks to move too">
      <p>
        The week count is the age of the pregnancy from the last period. Adjusting the due
        date does not rewind or advance that age. 59 days is 8 weeks and 3 days at cycle 28
        and at cycle 32. Read the days-until card if you wanted the distance to the new date.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Typing the due date as the last period">
      <p>
        The first field is the first day of bleeding, not the date you hope to deliver. Put
        8 October 2026 in that field and the page will project a due date about 280 days
        after October, which is a different pregnancy. The tool cannot tell those two dates
        apart. You can.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="This is not a prenatal visit">
      <p>
        No scan, no diagnosis, no fundal height. If a clinician has already dated the
        pregnancy from an ultrasound, that date is the one to plan around. This page is the
        arithmetic you can rerun when the cycle length was wrong.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="next">
    <h2>What should you open next? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A pregnancy due date calculator versus the alternatives keeps the cycle length on the
      page and recomputes the due date, the weeks, and the trimester dates together. A span
      of calendar days that is not a pregnancy belongs in the
      <a href={diffHref}>date difference calculator</a>. An exact age from a birth date
      belongs in the <a href={ageHref}>age calculator</a>. Neither of those reads a cycle
      length, and this page does not try to compute an age in years.
    </p>
    <p>
      Come back when the count date changes. The same last period and the same cycle produce
      a new week count in one edit. Leave the cycle at 28 only when 28 is the cycle you
      mean. A different length should be visible next to the due date, which is what the
      note is for.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Check 1 January 2026 →</a>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }
</style>
